@@ -148,6 +148,23 @@ Every option is optional except `password`. The `name` option is worth calling o
 > [!NOTE]
 > The `secure: true` option tells the browser to only store and send the cookie over HTTPS. When developing locally over plain HTTP, compliant browsers (notably Safari and iOS, and Chrome on some local domains) silently drop the cookie, so the session will not persist. Set `cookie: { secure: false }` during local development to work around this.
 
+### Rotate the password
+
+To rotate `password` without logging out existing visitors, move the current password to `extraUnsealPasswords` and set the new one as `password`:
+
+```js
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_NEW, // used to seal every new session
+  extraUnsealPasswords: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD], // only used to open sessions
+});
+```
+
+- Extra passwords are only used to open sessions. New and resealed sessions always use `password`.
+- Cookie sessions sealed with an extra password are resealed with `password` the next time they are read. Sessions read from the session header are not resealed, so they keep working with the old password until they expire.
+- Remove an old password from the list once the sessions it sealed have expired. Until then it stays valid for opening sessions. A session with neither `maxAge` nor `idleTimeout` never expires, so its old password has to stay in the list until you accept signing those visitors out.
+- Each extra password needs the same entropy as `password`, at least 32 random characters. An invalid entry makes `getSession` throw.
+- Each extra password is one more key derivation for every request that is not opened by an earlier password. Keep the list to two or three passwords.
+
 ## Expiration
 
 Sessions have two independent expiration controls, and you can use either or both:
